MODEL 82 — BUFFER SOLUTION SIMULATOR

Core objective

Build an interactive laboratory showing how buffer solutions resist changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added.

This model must provide a distinct learning experience from the titration laboratory, even though both may use acid–base chemistry.

A. 3D solution environment

Create a vessel containing an appropriate weak-acid/conjugate-base or weak-base/conjugate-acid buffer system.

Display:

- The weak acid or weak base.
- Its conjugate partner.
- Added acid or base species.
- A pH indicator or digital pH readout.
- A buffer-capacity or resistance-to-pH-change visualization.
- A compact concentration-composition display.

Use distinguishable, consistently labelled molecular or ionic representations.

B. Buffer selection

Include a scientifically suitable buffer example, such as an acetic acid/acetate buffer, where reliable equilibrium data are available.

Allow the user to adjust the ratio of buffer components and their total concentration.

Do not label an arbitrary mixture of any acid and base as a buffer. Ensure the selected system has a valid conjugate pair and an appropriate pH range.

C. Functional controls

Implement:

1. Buffer System Selector: choose among supported buffer systems.
2. Weak Component Concentration: adjust the relevant weak acid or weak base concentration.
3. Conjugate Component Concentration: adjust its conjugate partner.
4. Acid Addition: add a defined amount of strong acid to the system.
5. Base Addition: add a defined amount of strong base to the system.
6. pH Display: calculate and update the solution pH.
7. Composition Display: show the remaining buffer components after addition.
8. Buffer Capacity Comparison: compare two defined buffer compositions under the same acid or base addition.
9. Reset: restore the initial composition.

All additions must be reflected in species amounts, volume where applicable, concentration calculations and pH.

D. Scientific calculations

For a suitable weak-acid/conjugate-base buffer in the applicable region, use:

[
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm{p}K_a+
\log_{10}\left(\frac{[\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}\right)
]

Use appropriate stoichiometric neutralization before calculating the resulting buffer ratio when strong acid or base is added.

Do not apply the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation outside its valid range or when the buffer components have been exhausted. Implement a suitable equilibrium calculation or clearly explain any approximation used near the limits.

For a weak-base buffer, use the corresponding chemically appropriate relationship.

E. Educational feedback

Explain:

- The role of a conjugate acid–base pair.
- Why added acid consumes the basic buffer component.
- Why added base consumes the acidic buffer component.
- Why pH changes relatively little within the useful buffer range.
- How the ratio of conjugate components influences pH.
- How total buffer concentration affects resistance to added acid or base.
- Why a buffer has finite capacity and cannot resist unlimited additions.

Display the pH change before and after each addition. Avoid presenting buffer capacity as a universal number independent of the defined system and conditions.

